  "slug": "therapy-chronic-6e70",
  "summary": "Model-based predictions of relapse and dosing schedules are now emerging across CLL, CML and drug-persister settings.",
  "synthesis": "The common thread is the use of reduced or hybrid dynamical models to turn treatment failure into testable predictions about dosing and relapse. The CLL framework couples patient-specific ibrutinib pharmacokinetics with stochastic Boolean gene regulatory dynamics, using single-cell data to model relapse and screen BTK-associated targets. The CML study uses a minimal two-equation model with the immune compartment reduced by quasi-steady-state, reporting that leukemic fitness predicts treatment-free remission and can guide adaptive therapy. The persister model is phenomenological, describing plasticity-driven drug persistence across a continuum and predicting an optimal drug concentration and drug holiday schedule. What is new is the explicit pharmacokinetic-to-regulatory coupling in the CLL work and the fitness-based prediction of treatment-free remission in CML. All results are model-derived predictions; the summaries report no experimental validation beyond these stated predictions.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-01"
}
